**What's wrong with the draft:** `dueDate(new Date())` and `addDays(new Date(), 30)` read the clock at two different moments, so the two sides can differ by a few milliseconds. That is why it fails intermittently, and midnight is only one case where the values can diverge. The test depends on the wall clock when it should depend only on the function's logic.

**Fix:** use fixed input dates and literal expected dates, so the test is deterministic and doesn't reuse the logic it's checking.

```js
// test/billing/due.test.js
const { dueDate } = require('../../src/billing/due');

describe('dueDate', () => {
  test('is 30 days after the issue date', () => {
    const issueDate = new Date(2024, 0, 15, 12, 0, 0);  // 15 Jan 2024, local noon
    const expected = new Date(2024, 1, 14, 12, 0, 0);   // 14 Feb 2024 (30 days later)
    expect(dueDate(issueDate)).toEqual(expected);
  });

  test('crosses a year boundary correctly', () => {
    const issueDate = new Date(2024, 11, 20, 12, 0, 0); // 20 Dec 2024
    const expected = new Date(2025, 0, 19, 12, 0, 0);   // 19 Jan 2025 (30 days later)
    expect(dueDate(issueDate)).toEqual(expected);
  });

  test('does not modify the issue date', () => {
    const issueDate = new Date(2024, 0, 15, 12, 0, 0);
    const before = issueDate.getTime();
    dueDate(issueDate);
    expect(issueDate.getTime()).toBe(before);
  });
});
```

If a test ever does need "today", use Jest's fake timers instead of the real clock:

```js
beforeEach(() => {
  jest.useFakeTimers().setSystemTime(new Date(2024, 0, 15, 12, 0, 0));
});
afterEach(() => {
  jest.useRealTimers();
});
```

